  "tips": {
    "greetings": "💡 \"La bès?\" (How are you?) with \"La bès, hamdullah\" (Fine, thank God) is the classic Moroccan exchange. French is widely spoken.",
    "food": "💡 Eat with your right hand. Bread (\"khobz\") accompanies every meal — use it to scoop tagine. Mint tea is a welcome ritual.",
    "directions": "💡 Petit taxis are cheap within cities (metered). Grand taxis go between cities. Negotiate price before getting in.",
    "numbers": "💡 Currency is Moroccan Dirham (MAD). Cash is essential — many souks and riads are cash only.",
    "emergency": "💡 Dial 190 (police), 150 (fire), 141 (SAMU medical). Brigade Touristique for tourist assistance.",
    "shopping": "The first price in a souk is an opening. Counter lower, and stay polite.",
    "medical": "Call 141 for an ambulance. Drink bottled water.",
    "immigration": "Have your passport and the riad address in French or Arabic.",
    "hotel": "A riad sits inside the medina. Pin the door, because taxis stop at the gate.",
    "transport": "Agree the petit-taxi fare, or ask for the meter, before the car moves."
  },
  "pronGuide": {
    "title": "Darija Sounds",
    "tips": [
      "Darija (Moroccan Arabic) borrows heavily from French and Berber — you'll hear French words mixed in constantly",
      "The letters gh and kh are guttural — similar to the Spanish J or French R",
      "Double consonants are pronounced longer, e.g. \"bzzaf\" (a lot) has a long Z",
      "The H's in Moroccan words are real consonants — always pronounce them"
    ]
  },
